\begin{document}
\section{Bruges: The Staple and the Italian Factor}

Bruges rose as the northern terminus of the Italian
maritime route,
its fairs clearing goods from the Baltic, England, and the Mediterranean.
The Italian nations kept permanent fondachi in the city
and dominated the money market by mid-century.

Unlike Venice, Bruges never organised the trade it hosted.
Its decline after 1490 owed as much to the silting of the Zwin
as to the rerouting of the Portuguese spice trade to Antwerp.

\end{document}

